# Who must approve what in this repo.
#
# The ruleset on the default branch requires one approving review and
# code-owner review. Code owners are listed below by path; every path NOT
# listed has no owner, so the code-owner requirement is satisfied vacuously
# and the single required approval can come from anyone who can review --
# including delegate-reviewer[bot], whose approvals are real approvals.
#
# So the list below is the whole boundary. A path on it needs a human; a path
# off it can merge on a bot approval alone.
#
# gp-contrib is used because it is the only team with write access to this
# repo; gp-secops has read only, and GitHub silently ignores code owners
# without write access.
#
# ## The test
#
# Scoped access that does not touch security posture does not need a human.
# Applying it means asking not "is this application code?" but "what
# credential can this reach?" -- and in this repo the answer is often not the
# tree the file lives in. Three of the entries below are here only because
# something outside their directory reaches in.
#
# ## The trees that define the posture
#
# `/.github/` -- all of it. OIDC trust keys on the ref, not the filename, so a
# workflow *added* on main can assume the deploy roles. This file is here for
# the obvious reason: it decides who must approve.
#
# `/delegate/` -- all of it, not just `agents/pr-reviewer.ts`. The framework,
# the lambda dispatcher, the worker entrypoint and the subagent prompts all
# shape what the reviewer does, and every delegate agent shares one worker
# runtime and one GitHub App installation token with write access to the
# product repos. Letting the reviewer's approval merge a change to the
# reviewer collapses the boundary between coder and reviewer.
#
# `/deploy/`, `/deploy-org/`, `/deploy-workbench/` -- all three Pulumi
# projects, not just the role and permission-set files. A new component that
# mints an IAM role is one `index.ts` import away.
#
# ## The paths that reach a credential from outside their tree
#
# `/utils/` -- `deploy/components/identity-center/policies.ts` imports
# `bedrockInvokeResources` from `utils/bedrock-models.ts`. That array *is* the
# permission set's resource ARN list. It reads like a constants file and
# edits like an IAM policy.
#
# `/scripts/`, `/run-script.ts` -- `.github/workflows/deploy-workbench.yml`
# triggers on `scripts/enable-bedrock-models.ts` and runs `APPLY=1 npm run
# script enable-bedrock-models` under a deploy role; that script accepts model
# provider terms on GoodParty's behalf. `run-script.ts` dynamically imports
# anything in `scripts/`, so the entrypoint is generic even though the
# workflow trigger is not.
#
# `/package.json`, `/package-lock.json`, `/tsconfig.json` -- one
# `package.json` builds every image, so a dependency added for bugboss also
# installs into the delegate worker that holds the GitHub App token.
# `tsconfig.json` governs the same `tsc` that compiles `delegate/`.
#
# `/.dockerignore` -- the build context for both images.
#
# ## BugBoss: open by default, with its boundaries owned
#
# Most of `bugboss/` is application code and merges on a bot approval. Four
# parts are not application code:
#
# `bugboss/toolapi/` -- the agent's tool surface, and `token.ts` in it is "the
# reason a fully compromised agent can still only corrupt one record". A new
# file here is a new capability for a process that reads attacker-writable log
# lines.
# `bugboss/dispatcher/` -- mints the child agent's AWS credentials. Its own
# comment names the agent role as "the containment boundary".
# `bugboss/slack-app-manifest.yaml` -- the OAuth scopes, checked in expressly
# so they stay reviewable.
# `bugboss/Dockerfile` -- runs after the credentials step in `deploy.yml`.
#
# BugBoss holds GitHub App credentials and can open PRs, but those PRs face
# the target repo's own review gates, and its AWS reach is fixed in
# `deploy/components/bugboss.ts`, owned above. Application code cannot widen
# it.
#
# ## The reviewer's own criteria
#
# `CLAUDE.md`, at the root and in any directory. `delegate/agents/pr-reviewer.ts`
# step 2 tells the reviewer to read "the repo's root `CLAUDE.md` -- authoritative
# for conventions" and any `CLAUDE.md` in a directory the PR touches, and the
# scout subagent is told the same. So these files are an input to the judgement
# that this change is about making sufficient. A bot-approved edit here changes
# what the reviewer will accept on every later PR, which is the one kind of
# change that compounds.
#
# This is the only file-pattern entry that is not a named singleton, and it is
# a pattern rather than a directory because the files it covers are scattered
# by design. It costs a human on a bugboss documentation edit, which is the
# right side to err on.
#
# ## Rules for editing this list
#
# Scope by directory. A file-scoped gate leaves the same hole one file over:
# adding `deploy/components/evil.ts` touches no file-scoped path, so it would
# need no human review. This repo has already been bitten by exactly that --
# see the `job_workflow_ref` comment in
# `deploy/components/ci-roles/policies.ts`. The four file-scoped entries below
# are singletons referenced by name from an owned path, where there is no
# "one file over" to add.
#
# Before adding a path to the unowned side, check what executes it. The
# ordering in `deploy.yml` -- install, test and build strictly before the
# credentials step -- is load-bearing for every unowned path in this repo, and
# is the only reason an unowned `bugboss/**/*.test.ts` is not an
# admin-credentialled shell.
#
# Narrowing this list is a security change. It is also, by construction, a
# change to a path on the list, so it needs a human.

/.github/                        @thegoodparty/gp-contrib
/delegate/                       @thegoodparty/gp-contrib
/deploy/                         @thegoodparty/gp-contrib
/deploy-org/                     @thegoodparty/gp-contrib
/deploy-workbench/               @thegoodparty/gp-contrib
/scripts/                        @thegoodparty/gp-contrib
/utils/                          @thegoodparty/gp-contrib
/run-script.ts                   @thegoodparty/gp-contrib
/package.json                    @thegoodparty/gp-contrib
/package-lock.json               @thegoodparty/gp-contrib
/tsconfig.json                   @thegoodparty/gp-contrib
/.dockerignore                   @thegoodparty/gp-contrib

/bugboss/toolapi/                @thegoodparty/gp-contrib
/bugboss/dispatcher/             @thegoodparty/gp-contrib
/bugboss/github.ts               @thegoodparty/gp-contrib
/bugboss/slack-app-manifest.yaml @thegoodparty/gp-contrib
/bugboss/Dockerfile              @thegoodparty/gp-contrib

# Last, so it wins over the tree rules above. Same owner either way, so the
# ordering is for readers rather than for GitHub.
/CLAUDE.md                       @thegoodparty/gp-contrib
**/CLAUDE.md                     @thegoodparty/gp-contrib
